 \section{Claim Boundary and Contributions}
 EchoForge is intentionally narrower than a measured radar collection. It publishes public-proxy signatures and detector-facing products with explicit uncertainty and validation tiers, while keeping generated arrays, solver outputs, and measured raw files out of Git. The radar and detection framing follows standard signal-processing and CFAR practice rather than a sensor-surrogate claim \cite{skolnik2008,richards2014,rohling1983}. The paper uses repository-compatible legacy identifiers only where required for tooling; the paper-facing positive class is named \emph{fixed-wing pusher-prop public proxy}. No measured-platform truth, proprietary-equivalent behavior, or classified fidelity is claimed for any object, platform, or sensor.
+
+\subsection{Core Experiment in One Paragraph}
+The main experiment is intentionally simple. We compare detector branches, prior ML, and the accepted human-engineered layered-fusion comparator against one EI sparse-fusion challenger. All methods use the same group-locked split. Train/CV rows are used for candidate discovery, calibration, and thresholding; the blind holdout is scored once after the lock. The result that matters is not a leaderboard of every diagnostic metric. It is whether EI improves the low-false-alarm operating point over accepted fusion practice while preserving the claim boundary.
